<setup>

  <selectFromSequence name="a" from="16" to="32" step="8"/>
  <selectFromSequence name="b" from="30" to="40" step="5"/>
  <math name="c" simplify>$b/$a^2</math>
  <math name="dx" simplify>$a/4</math>

  <function name="v">$c*($a-x)^2</function>

  <math name="L4">$dx * ($$v(0) + $$v($dx) + $$v(2*$dx) + $$v(3*$dx))</math>
  <math name="R4">$dx * ($$v($dx) + $$v(2*$dx) + $$v(3*$dx) + $$v(4*$dx))</math>
  <math name="T4">($L4 + $R4)/2</math>
  <math name="M4">$dx * ($$v($a+$dx/2) + $$v($a+3*$dx/2) + $$v($a+5*$dx/2) + $$v($a+7*$dx/2))</math>
  <math name="S8">(2*$M4 + $T4)/3</math>

</setup>

  <p>
    Let <m>r(t)</m> represent the rate at which water is draining from a large tank (measured in liters per minute) at time <m>t</m> in minutes.  The following table shows some of the values of <m>v(t)</m>.
  </p>

      <tabular bottom="minor" top="minor">
        <row bottom="minor" halign="center">
          <cell><m>t</m></cell>
          <cell><m>0</m></cell>
          <cell><m><math simplify>$dx/2</math></m></cell>
          <cell><m><math simplify>2*$dx/2</math></m></cell>
          <cell><m><math simplify>3*$dx/2</math></m></cell>
          <cell><m><math simplify>4*$dx/2</math></m></cell>
        </row>
        <row halign="center">
          <cell><m>r(t)</m></cell>
          <cell><m><number displayDecimals="2">$$v(0)</number></m></cell>
          <cell><m><number displayDecimals="2">$$v($dx/2)</number></m></cell>
          <cell><m><number displayDecimals="2">$$v(2*$dx/2)</number></m></cell>
          <cell><m><number displayDecimals="2">$$v(3*$dx/2)</number></m></cell>
          <cell><m><number displayDecimals="2">$$v(4*$dx/2)</number></m></cell>
        </row>
      </tabular>

      <tabular bottom="minor" top="minor">
        <row bottom="minor" halign="center">
          <cell><m>t</m></cell>
          <cell><m><math simplify>5*$dx/2</math></m></cell>
          <cell><m><math simplify>6*$dx/2</math></m></cell>
          <cell><m><math simplify>7*$dx/2</math></m></cell>
          <cell><m><math simplify>8*$dx/2</math></m></cell>
          <cell><m><math simplify>9*$dx/2</math></m></cell>
        </row>
        <row halign="center">
          <cell><m>r(t)</m></cell>
          <cell><m><number displayDecimals="2">$$v(5*$dx/2)</number></m></cell>
          <cell><m><number displayDecimals="2">$$v(6*$dx/2)</number></m></cell>
          <cell><m><number displayDecimals="2">$$v(7*$dx/2)</number></m></cell>
          <cell><m><number displayDecimals="2">$$v(8*$dx/2)</number></m></cell>
          <cell><m><math simplify>0</math></m></cell>
        </row>
      </tabular>

<p>
  We are going to use various Riemann sums with <m><math simplify>4</math></m> subintervals to generate the Simpson's Rule estimate for
  <me>
    \int_{0}^{$a} r(t) \, dt
  </me>
</p>

<p>
  <ol marker="a.">
    <li>

      <p>
        For this data, fill in the following blanks to identify the values of <m>\Delta t</m> and <m>t_i</m> for <m>i = 0</m> to <m>i = 4</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>\Delta t = </m><answer>$dx</answer>
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>t_0 = </m><answer>0</answer>, <m>t_1 = </m><answer>$dx</answer>, <m>t_2 = </m><answer>2*$dx</answer>,
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>t_3 = </m><answer>3*$dx</answer>, <m>t_4 = </m><answer>4*$dx</answer>
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>
        Compute the left and right Riemann sums on <m>[0,$a]</m> with <m>4</m> subintervals, as well as the Trapezoid Rule for <m>4</m> subintervals.
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>L_4 = </m><answer allowedErrorInNumbers="0.2" allowedErrorIsAbsolute> $L4</answer>
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>R_4 = </m><answer allowedErrorInNumbers="0.2" allowedErrorIsAbsolute> $R4</answer>
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>T_4 = </m><answer allowedErrorInNumbers="0.2" allowedErrorIsAbsolute> $T4</answer>
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>
        Find the value of the midpoint Riemann sum on <m>[0,$a]</m> with <m>4</m> subintervals.
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>M_4 = </m><answer allowedErrorInNumbers="0.2" allowedErrorIsAbsolute> $M4</answer>
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>
        Finally, determine the value of the Simpson's Rule estimate for <m>\int_{0}^{$a} r(t) \, dt</m> with <m>8</m> subintervals.
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>S_8 = </m><answer name="enteredS8" allowedErrorInNumbers="0.2" allowedErrorIsAbsolute> $S8</answer>
      </p>
    </li>

    <li>
      <p>
        Complete the following sentences to explain the meaning of the estimate you found for <m>\int_{0}^{$a} r(t) \, dt</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Since the units on <m>r(t)</m> are <choiceInput inline name="c1">
          <choice>?</choice>
          <choice>liters</choice>
          <choice>liters per minute</choice>
          <choice>minutes</choice>
        </choiceInput> and the units on <m>\Delta t</m> are <choiceInput inline name="c2">
          <choice>?</choice>
          <choice>liters</choice>
          <choice>liters per minute</choice>
          <choice>minutes</choice>
        </choiceInput>, when we compute a product such as <m>r(t_2) \cdot \Delta t</m> in a Riemann sum, the units on <m>r(t_2) \cdot \Delta t</m> are
        <choiceInput inline name="c3">
          <choice>?</choice>
          <choice>liters</choice>
          <choice>liters per minute</choice>
          <choice>minutes</choice>
        </choiceInput>.  This means that <m>\int_{0}^{$a} r(t) \, dt \approx</m><mathInput disabled>$enteredS8</mathInput> estimates the <choiceInput inline name="c4">
          <choice>?</choice>
          <choice>total water (in liters) that drained from the tank on <m>[0, <math simplify>$a</math>]</m></choice>
          <choice>total time (in minutes) it took the tank to drain <math simplify>$a</math> liters</choice>
          <choice>average rate (in liters per minute) the tank is draining on <m>[0, <math simplify>$a</math>]</m></choice>
        </choiceInput>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <answer><award><when>
          $c1.selectedIndices=3 and $c2.selectedIndices=4 and $c3.selectedIndices=2 and $c4.selectedIndices=2
        </when></award></answer>
      </p>
    </li>

  </ol>
</p>